hi there Steve Kaufman here today I'm  back in Vancouver after having attended  the long Fest the festival of languages  in Montreal remember and today by the  way I'm going to talk about  comprehensible input as the key driver  of language acquisition a major theme at  the conference in Montreal but remember  if you enjoy these videos please  subscribe we were lucky we were very  fortunate to have in montreal stephen  krashen who has written widely on the  subject of language language acquisition  and stresses that there is only one way  in which we acquire language and that is  through comprehensible input messages  that are meaningful to us he went so far  as to say that the the content that we  use should be compelling and so there  was his conference was very interesting  there was there were other speakers  presenters who dealt with this theme for  example Paul Doucet who is a teacher in  New York state of belief of languages  who is a wonderful Pali God himself and  he also stressed the importance of  comprehensible input ci as it's referred  to and how this revolution in language  learning and language instruction is  still largely a concept that people pay  lip service to whereas in schools kids  are primarily taught through the old  grammar instruction you know system and  he explains some of the difficulties in  introducing sort of CI comprehensible  input based instruction he referred to  tt-rs  teaching proficiency through reading and  storytelling which is a popular  technique that is gaining in popularity  these are all things that you can google  for TP RS you can google for stephen  krashen comprehensible input and so  forth but today i want to talk about it  from the perspective of me the learner  so people often say comprehensible input  but when you're starting in the language  you don't understand anything now the  theory is if you read material that is  always a little bit difficult for you  perhaps initially you have a very short  lesson with a vocabulary list or  something  and gradually you you deal with material  that has a small number of new words and  gradually by reading extensively and by  listening you will eventually acquire  the language it isn't quite that simple  in reality because if you are constantly  dealing with material that has only a  few unknown words you will take forever  to learn the language so you have to in  least in my experience I try to speed  things up so I will of course in the old  days I would deal with with readers  where there was a vocabulary list the  difficulty is if you review a vocabulary  list before reading something  essentially you don't remember much in  fact there is research that shows if two  groups of students one group knows what  the story is about has read it in  English or something and the other group  is given a vocabulary list it's the  first group those who are familiar with  the subject matter that will understand  more reviewing a vocabulary list ahead  of reading something has very little  impact on our comprehension  so I've always said the sort of a major  revolution has been reading online or on  your iPad with access to an online  dictionary you can quickly look  something up it doesn't tell you the  full meaning of the word it may even  give you a slightly erroneous sense of  what the word means but in most cases it  allows you to get through the content  and of course if you're on link you can  save that for later review and of course  the word on your screen changes color  which is a form of reminder helping you  notice the fact that this is a word that  you've seen so the use of modern  technology enables us to access material  with a higher percentage of unknown  words it needn't be just 5% unknown  words I'm very happy with material of  this 10 or 15 percent unknown  once heard up to 30 or 40% it becomes  difficult so obviously when you start  though it's all unknown so this is  another important point to me as a  language learner you know I know in my  presentation I talked about the very  steep start to language learning I  called it the blade of the ups to upside  down hockey stick where you are  struggling to kind of get a grip of the  language you have a sense of  satisfaction that you're able to make  some sense of it you're starting to hear  where words and the new words begin and  you're struggling and then after that  there's this long period of acquiring  all the words you need to get to some  form of comfortable ability in the  language call it fluency call it B - so  during this first period the content  that you are using the input material  can't be that compelling you know on its  own it's not something that you will  read in your own language so this is  important to remember I am compelled to  work on my Greek or my Hebrew many  stories not because they are so  tremendously interesting but because I  am compelled to discover this language  so my color tolerance for content that  is not maybe intrinsically so  interesting my tolerance for that is  higher and so I work my way through  these the other thing I do and this is  an important point I read and listen  many many times mark and I my son Mark  who is the chief operating officer at  link and co-founder with me he showed  what he did in his account in both  Spanish and Italian and you know I think  you can basically all of the audio from  the lessons you can move it to a  playlist so that you can listen to it  and the playlist tracks how many times  you listen and I saw that mark had been  listening to his Spanish lessons 40 50  times and I hadn't really looked at it  but then I checked my own playlist in  Greek and sure enough I've been  listening to those many stories 50 40 35  times and so obviously the 30th time you  listen it's no longer come  you're not you know curious to know how  it ends but we are compelled at least I  am I am compelled because I want to get  a better handle on I want to understand  it better I want to go over these words  again I have saved certain phrases that  I want to get ahead a lot so I am  motivated by my desire to get past that  very steep beginning in the language get  to a level where I don't have to listen  30 times where I can access material  that is interesting of itself it could  be the history of the country or some  news article so during that period of  course you have to read and listen many  many times and every time I listen again  I noticed something else I notice a turn  of phrase or a structure or something  that I hadn't noticed before so  compelling input comprehensible input I  think can mean different things at  different stages in your learning and I  think at an initial stage you are able  using online dictionaries or a system  like link to plow your way through  material that might have 15 20 percent  unknown words in it course initially  it's 100 percent and as you progress you  listen less and less often to the same  material you're moving more quickly on  the new material and and I think in this  issue of repeating verses going to new  material I always say don't don't I  don't wait until I have totally aced  something before I move on in fact very  often I move on to the next story in the  next dirty story in the next story  and then I go back again to the  beginning because the brain as I often  quote manfred spitzer the great german  neuroscientist  who said that the brain requires  repetition and novelty so you can repeat  and repeat but after a while the  repetition isn't doing anything so you  got to give the brain something new but  then you can go back and there's nothing  more enjoyable than going back to  something that was very difficult two  months earlier and now has become easier  so comprehensible input input is what  drives our language acquisition that's  not to say that we don't occasion  further grammar or have a teacher give  us a little explanation again as Paul  Doucet said in his presentation if an  issue of grammar comes up in a TP RS  classroom  he says you get this five second spot of  grammar and then you go back to the  story to the input and and the  expectation is not that this explanation  of grammar should then be remembered and  known and mastered it simply helps you  get through the story and in this way  the story the content is gradually  helping your brain get accustomed to the  language get accustomed to the  structures in the language acquire the  vocabulary of the language so  comprehensible input compelling input  compelling for different reasons at  different stages that's what drives  language learning and so there you have  it I look forward to your comments and  thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]
 

